                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Arr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                      %mo ×
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Arr(D) \to D\times D
                is faithful—such an extension to a functor is necessarily unique.
              %p
                So we may regard the component function of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
                as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-functor"} 0-functor
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi sk
                        %mn 0
                      %mi C
                      %mo =
                      %mi Obj
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mrow
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mi Arr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\eta \colon \mathbf{sk}_0 C = Obj(C) \to D^{\Delta[1]} = Arr(D)
              %p
                from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
                on the set of objects of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+category"} arrow category
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n=2)
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudonatural+transformation"} pseudonatural transformation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
                between (strict, say, for ease of of notation)
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 2
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_1,F_2 : C \to D
                between (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"}> strict
                , for simplicity)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
                is in components a 1-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                that functorially assigns pseudonaturality squares:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                      %mo :
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi x
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi γ
                                        %mn 1
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi y
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi γ
                                        %mn 2
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi z
                        %mo )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ↦
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi F
                                  %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi x
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo →
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi η
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi x
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi F
                                  %mn 2
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi x
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi F
                                        %mn 1
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mi f
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mo ⇙
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi η
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi f
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi F
                                        %mn 2
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mi f
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi F
                                  %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi y
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo →
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi η
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi y
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi F
                                  %mn 2
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi y
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi F
                                        %mn 1
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mi g
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mo ⇙
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi η
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi g
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi F
                                        %mn 2
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mi g
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi F
                                  %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi z
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %munder
                                  %mo →
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi η
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi z
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi F
                                  %mn 2
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi z
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\eta :
                      \\left(
                      \\array{
                      x
                      \\\
                      \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\gamma_1}}
                      \\\
                      y
                      \\\
                      \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\gamma_2}}
                      \\\
                      z
                      }
                      \\right)
                      \\;\;\;
                      \\mapsto
                      \\;\;\;
                      \\left(
                      \\array{
                      F_1(x) &amp;\stackrel{\eta(x)}{\to}&amp; F_2(x)
                      \\\
                      {}^{\mathllap{F_1(f)}}\downarrow
                      \&amp;\swArrow_{\eta(f)}&amp;
                      \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{F_2(f)}}
                      \\\
                      F_1(y) &amp;\stackrel{\eta(y)}{\to}&amp; F_2(y)
                      \\\
                      {}^{\mathllap{F_1(g)}}\downarrow
                      \&amp;\swArrow_{\eta(g)}&amp;
                      \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{F_2(g)}}
                      \\\
                      F_1(z) &amp;\underset{\eta(z)}{\to}&amp; F_2(z)
                      }
                      \\right)
              %p We may regard this as a 1-functor
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi sk
                        %mn 1
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi Arr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\eta : \mathbf{sk}_1 C \to Arr(D)
              %p
                from the underlying 1-category of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                to the arrow category of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting"} pasting
                of such squares (see
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
                for details).
              %p
                Again, saying that this 1-functor extends to a 2-functor from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                to the arrow 2-category of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                says precisely that these components form a pseudonatural transformation, and any such extension is unique when it exists since the forgetful 2-functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Arr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                      %mo ×
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Arr(D)\to D\times D
                is locally faithful.
            %li#SchematicsIn3d
              %p
                %strong
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n=3)
                – A transformation between 3-functors is in components a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functor
                that sends
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                to cyclinders in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                = succeed "-row" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n=3)
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Schreiber2006"} Schreiber 2006
              %p
                %img{:alt => "components of transformations", :src => "/nlab/files/TwistedQFTAndHolographicCats.png", :width => "550"}/
              %p
                The pseudonaturality condition on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi η
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              %p
                %img{:alt => "2-pseudonaturality", :src => "/nlab/files/natthree.gif", :width => "550"}/
              %p
                and the fact that there are only identity 3-morphisms in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                implies that this already uniquely extends to a 2-functor
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi Arr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\eta \colon C \to Arr(D)
                      \\,,
              %p
                where on the right we have the 2-category whose objects are morphisms in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                and whose 2-morphisms are cylinders bounded by these squares.
          %h3#Formalizations Formalizations
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-categories"} strict ∞-categories
            modeled as globular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-categories"} strict ∞-categories
            we have the following simple statement of the general principle.
          %p
          .num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Str
                    %mi n
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C,D \in Str n Cat
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_1, F_2 \colon C \to D
              two strict
              = succeed "-functors," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "mations" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfor"} transfor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⇒
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta \colon F_1 \Rightarrow F_2
              which are in components given by
              = succeed "-functors" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\eta \colon C \to D^{G_1}
            %p
              are entirely specified by their underlying
              = succeed "-functors" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\eta \colon C_{n-1} \to D^{G_1}
                    \\,.
            %p
          %p
            For weak
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            is specified by its values on
            = succeed "-morphisms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "-morphisms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "-morphisms." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            One way to bring the general case into the above simple form is to invoke models by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-strict+%E2%88%9E-categories"} semi-strict ∞-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simpson%27s+conjecture"} Simpson's conjecture
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"} ∞-category
            has a model in which everything is strict except possibly the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identities"} identities
            and their unitalness
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+law"} coherence law
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is such a semistrict model of an
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{n-1}
            is an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicategory"} semicategory
            and the transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\eta : C_{n-1} \to D^{\Delta[1]}
          %p
            is an
            = succeed "-functor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            is guaranteed also to respect the weak identities in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            in some way, but that way is not so easy to formalize.)
          %p
            More generally, for any algebraic notion of weak
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-dimensional\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "-semicategory." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
          %h2#ApplicationsInQFT Application in functorial QFT
          %p
            In the context of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+functorial+field+theory"}> extended
            )
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+field+theory"} functorial field theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+functorial+field+theory"} twisted functorial field theory
            (or
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+functorial+field+theory"} relative functorial field theory
          %p
            For instance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            one models
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theories"} topological quantum field theories
            as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-functor"} (∞,n)-functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (∞,n)-category of cobordisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                    %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z : Bord_{n}^S \to \mathcal{C}
          %p
            (where the superscript
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is to remind us that this may be
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} structure
          %p
            It follows that with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_1, Z_2
            two such
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B : Z_1 \Rightarrow Z_2
            does look in components itself like an QFT – which is
            %em twisted
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_2
            in some sense (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => ""} below
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
          %p
            More specifically, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,n)-category
            with tensor unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            there is the trivial FQFT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            given by the constant
            = succeed "-functor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Bord
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1} : Bord_n \to \mathcal{C}
          %p One can see in examples that the transformations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  B \colon Z \Rightarrow \mathbf{1}
          %p
            encode
            %em boundary conditions
            on cobordisms with boundary for the theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            one uses
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+transformation"} canonical transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta : C_{n-1} \to D^{I}
            that are required to be natural only with respect to the invertible
            = succeed "-morphisms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            For the case of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
            and 2-dimensional cobordisms without any extra structure, a detailed version of these statements are given in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#SchommerPries2009"} Schommer-Pries 2009
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=3
            and the holographic relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+model"} Reshetikhin-Turaev model
            and rational 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FFRS-formalism"} FFRS-formalism
            some remarks are in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Schreiber2007"} Schreiber 2007
          %p
            In the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            such kinds of relations between
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            \. The degree to which this principle has been formalized and the degree to which this formalization has been verified varies greatly. Examples include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The boundary theory of 3-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                is the 2-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW-model"} WZW-model
                (cf.
                = succeed ")." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} AdS3-CFT2 and CS-WZW correspondence
              %p This is probably the oldest known holographic relation between QFTs.
            %li
              %p
                In the 2-d QFT called the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
                with target the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
                coming from a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
                the boundary 3-point function computes the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
                of the classical system described by that Poisson manifold.
              %p
                (This was in fact used implicitly by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
                to solve
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            %li
              %p
                The boundary theory of the 2-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
                QFT on a target space that is a complexification of a classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
                is the 1-dimensional QFT (=
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
                of this phase space.
              %p
                This is described at
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+via+the+A-model"} quantization via the A-model
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
                conjectures specifically a holographic relation between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
                on a 10-dimensional asymptotically
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti-de Sitter spacetime
                and a superconformal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
                on its asymptotic boundary. More generally, this specific construction is supposed to apply, with variations, in many different dimensions.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+functorial+field+theory"} twisted functorial field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory+and+physics"}> higher category theory and physics
                \.
          %h2#References References
          %p The above text and graphics follow:
          %ul
            %li#Schreiber2006
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                \: “A kind of Holography”, section 4 of:
                %em On 2D QFT – from Arrows to Disks
                (2006) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/schreiber/ad.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "/schreiber/files/Schreiber-ArrowsToDisks.pdf"} pdf
              %p
                presented as:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum 2-States: Sections of 2-vector bundles
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/programs/scientific/06-07/homotopy/highercat/index.html"} Higher categories and their applications
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/schreiber/atd.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "/schreiber/files/schreiber_2VectorSpaces_Jan2007.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Discussion with respect to actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} higher
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+cobordisms"} categories of cobordisms
          %ul
            %li#SchommerPries2009
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Schommer-Pries"}> Chris Schommer-Pries
              , pp. 65 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topological defects and classifying local topological field theories in low dimension
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Strings%2C+Fields%2C+Topology"} Oberwolfach Workshop, June 2009 – Strings, Fields, Topology
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SchommerPriesDefects.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            A formally comparatively well understood case of QFT holography is the relation between 3-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            and the 2-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW-model"} WZW-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+model"} Reshetikhin-Turaev model
            on the 3-dimensional side and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fuchs-Runkel-Schweigert+construction"} Fuchs-Runkel-Schweigert construction
            on the 2-dimensional side. For more on this see at:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} AdS3-CFT2 and CS-WZW correspondence
          %p Remarks on how the relation between Reshitikhin-Turaev and FSR seem to have an interpretation in terms transformations between 3-functors:
          %ul
            %li#Schreiber2007
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em Towards 2-functorial CFT
              (2007) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/08/dbranes_from_tin_cans_part_x.html"}> blog entry
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            There is it discussed how the basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            that in FSR formalism encodes a field insertion on, possibly, a defect line and encodes the disk amplitudes of the CFT is the string diagram Poincaré-dual to the cylinder in a 3-category of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-vector+space"} 3-vector space
          %p
            For references on the actual
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            in QFT, see there.
